cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Weighted Safety Refusal (WSR) in your work, please cite it as below."
title: "Weighted Safety Refusal (WSR): A Reference-Free, Severity-Weighted, Dual-Axis Metric for LLM Refusal Behavior"
abstract: >-
  WSR is a reference-free metric for evaluating how well a language model
  refuses harmful requests without over-refusing matched-benign ones. It reports
  three numbers: R (severity-weighted refusal rate on harmful prompts), A
  (severity-weighted appropriate-answer rate on matched-benign prompts), and CSS
  (the harmonic mean of R and A — a gaming-resistant Calibrated Safety Score).
  R and A carry 95% Wilson-score confidence intervals, and the conservative lower
  bound of the CSS interval is the headline number. Scores are computed offline
  from a verdicts file, so the methodology is independently reproducible. The
  released dataset ships 120 prompts across 6 categories (10 harmful + 10
  matched-benign each); the severity weight vector (methodology v2) is
  auditable and versioned.
type: software
